**Day 25: MySQL vs PostgreSQL Comparison**  

**Topic:**  
Comparison of MySQL and PostgreSQL databases, focusing on features, performance, licensing, and community support.  

**Learning Objectives:**  

• Understand key differences in data types between MySQL and PostgreSQL  
• Compare performance benchmarks for common operations  
• Evaluate use cases and licensing models for each database  

**Activities / Deliverables:**  

• Research and document feature comparisons in a table format  
• Prepare a Microsoft Word report summarizing findings  
• Present comparison results to mentor for feedback  

**Important Concepts:**  

• MySQL prioritizes speed and simplicity, while PostgreSQL emphasizes advanced features and standards compliance  
• PostgreSQL supports more complex data types (e.g., JSON, arrays) natively  
• Licensing: MySQL uses GPL, PostgreSQL uses permissive MIT-style license  

**SQL Syntax / Commands:**  

• `CREATE DATABASE` (similar syntax, but PostgreSQL supports additional parameters)  
• `SELECT` (PostgreSQL allows more advanced window functions)  

**Real-world Use Cases:**  

• MySQL: Web applications, high-traffic read-heavy workloads (e.g., WordPress)  
• PostgreSQL: Complex analytical queries, geospatial data, enterprise applications  

**Tools / Tech:**  

• MySQL Workbench  
• pgAdmin (PostgreSQL)  

**Summary:**  
Today's research highlighted fundamental differences between MySQL and PostgreSQL, including their design philosophies, feature sets, and optimal use cases. MySQL excels in read-heavy web applications, while PostgreSQL offers superior functionality for complex data operations.  

